; A `(scalar-or-ref-shape …)` slot reports the ARM the node's shape selected,
; not the union it desugars to. The shorthand stores an ordinary
; `union [<base> <ref>]` and matches as one — try each, first accept wins —
; but its two arms are disjoint by node shape: a number can only have meant
; the base, a symbol only the ref. So when nothing matches there is still a
; determined arm, and the diagnostic is that arm's own. Here the base is a
; bounded integer, and each rejected literal names the bound that refused
; it — the reason the bounds were declared — where the union's collapsed
; `union_no_branch_matched` only named two kinds.
;
; Matching is untouched: `:q 8` and a resolved `:q TILE` are clean exactly
; as before, and no document changes verdict, only the code it fails with.
(plugin :name p :version "1.0.0"
  (value-kind :name qty-value :underlying number
    :numeric (numeric-bounds :min 0 :max 16 :integer true))
  (value-kind :name thing-ref :underlying symbol
    :cross-ref (cross-ref :target thing))
  (value-kind :name qty
    :underlying scalar-or-ref
    :scalar-or-ref (scalar-or-ref-shape :base qty-value :ref thing-ref))
  (form :name thing
    (key :name name :type symbol :optional false))
  (form :name use
    (key :name q :type qty :optional false)))

(thing :name TILE)

(use :q 8)      ; the base arm, in range — clean
(use :q TILE)   ; the ref arm, resolved — clean
(use :q 32)     ; the base arm refused: above :max
(use :q -1)     ; the base arm refused: below :min
(use :q 1.5)    ; the base arm refused: not an integer
